<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">António de Oliveira Salazar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">António de Oliveira Salazar was the authoritarian leader of Portugal from 1932 to 1968, heading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> regime. He insisted on keeping Portugal's African colonies, treating them as overseas provinces. His refusal to decolonise led to long colonial wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, which contributed to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> after his regime fell in 1974.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar.jpg" alt="António de Oliveira Salazar" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel Alves de San Payo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ant%C3%B3nio_de_Oliveira_Salazar_portrait_(by_Manuel_Alves_San_Payo)_%E2%80%93_Lisboa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Salazar was born on 28 April 1889 in Vimieiro, near Santa Comba Dão, to a family of modest income. His father, a small landowner, had begun as an agricultural labourer and become manager for the Perestrelos, a wealthy landowning family. At eleven Salazar won a free place at the seminary in Viseu, where he studied from 1900 to 1908; he considered the priesthood but decided against it after receiving holy orders.</p><p>He studied law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coimbra">Coimbra</a> from 1910, graduated in 1914 with 19 points out of 20, and specialised in finance. He took the chairs of economic policy and finance in 1917 and received his doctorate the next year. His politics grew from Catholic views aroused by the First Republic's anti-clerical stance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the coup of 28 May 1926 Salazar entered government as finance minister with the support of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-carmona">Óscar Carmona</a> and balanced the budget within a year. He became Prime Minister in 1932 and recast the Ditadura Nacional as the corporatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a>, whose constitution of 1933 was co-written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcelo-caetano">Marcelo Caetano</a>. Its stated basis was Catholic social doctrine, but in practice its stability rested on suppressing dissent.</p><p>A secret police, founded in 1933 and reorganised in 1945 as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pide">PIDE</a>, monitored the population, applied censorship and suppressed opponents. Humberto Delgado, who challenged the regime in the 1958 presidential election, was assassinated by the PIDE in 1965. Salazar supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> and kept Portugal neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Portugal was a founding member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1949 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1955. Colonial war broke out in 1961, the year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In August 1968 Salazar suffered a cerebral haemorrhage, which most sources attribute to a fall from a chair. On 25 September President Américo Tomás dismissed him and appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcelo-caetano">Marcelo Caetano</a>. Those around him did not tell him he had been removed, and he died on 27 July 1970. He never married and had no known descendants.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> outlived him by four years and fell to a military coup in 1974, leading to a liberal-democratic constitution in 1976. Historians find a detached judgement difficult: his rule brought more stability and prosperity than most Portuguese then living had known, but at the expense of democracy and civil liberties.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “António de Oliveira Salazar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They became the last battlegrounds of Portuguese colonialism, defended in the colonial wars from 1961 by the Estado Novo regime of <b>António de Oliveira Salazar</b> and his successor Marcelo Caetano.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Promised maximum support by Portugal's <b>António de Oliveira Salazar</b> in September 1964, and knowing that Harold Wilson would not use force against white 'kith and kin', Smith rejected the five principles set out by Sir Alec Douglas-Home, and on 11 November 1965 his cabinet issued the unilateral declaration…</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dictators also ruled Poland after Józef Piłsudski’s coup of 1926, Portugal under <b>António de Oliveira Salazar</b>, Spain under Francisco Franco from 1939, much of the Balkans and the Baltic states, and Brazil under Getúlio Vargas from 1937.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Sodade&quot; refers to a different movement: the departure of a small part of the population as contract labourers to São Tomé during <b>António de Oliveira Salazar</b>'s authoritarian rule over Portugal and its colonies.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sodade">Sodade</a></li><li><blockquote>Unable to solve the financial crisis, it appointed the Coimbra economics professor <b>António de Oliveira Salazar</b> as finance minister in 1928, with control over all spending.</blockquote><a href="/state/ditadura-nacional">Ditadura Nacional</a></li><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1933, approved by plebiscite, turned the Ditadura Nacional into a corporatist regime under prime minister <b>António de Oliveira Salazar</b>, inspired by Catholic social teaching and the idea of an organic nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo (Portugal)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Freguesia de Vimieiro, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.39,-8.12&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Freguesia de Vimieiro on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of António de Oliveira Salazar"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4473 4098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4473 4098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.39,-8.12&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
